          <p>
            The figures are typical ranges; your appliances may differ. The best source is the nameplate sticker on each appliance, or a plug-in energy meter left on the fridge and freezer for a full day. For hardwired equipment, the nameplate on the motor or the furnace's data label gives the amps; multiply by the voltage for an approximate wattage. If you cannot find a figure, an electrician or HVAC technician can measure the starting current with a clamp meter in a few minutes, and that one number can save you from buying the wrong inverter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two columns in that table drive the purchase. The hardwired column tells you whether you need a panel connection, which affects which unit you buy and what the installation costs. The starting watts column tells you how much inverter headroom you need: add the running watts of everything that might be on together, then add the single largest starting surge on top. That sum must fit under the power station's continuous and surge ratings, or the inverter will shut down at exactly the moment you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be honest about the loads you are leaving off, too. Central air conditioning, electric resistance heat, an electric water heater and an electric range each draw thousands of watts for hours, and none of them belong on a battery plan. A damp basement is a gray area: a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a> pulls a few hundred watts and is worth running in short bursts during a long summer outage, but it is a tier-four load that should only get energy left over once the fridge and pumps are covered.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">STAGGER THE MOTORS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You control more of the surge than you think.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">You cannot control when a fridge compressor cycles on, but you can control when you start a microwave, a well pump or a dehumidifier. Avoid running two large motor loads at the same moment and you can often get away with a smaller inverter than the worst-case sum suggests.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="worked-examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Essential-Circuit Worked Examples
          </h2>

          <p>
            The three households below use the runtime rule from our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">main power station guide</a>: runtime in hours is roughly capacity in Wh times 0.85, divided by the average load in watts. The 0.85 covers inverter losses and the reserve the battery management system holds back. For daily energy, we add each load's watts multiplied by its hours, then divide by 0.85 to get the battery capacity actually needed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 1: Summer Storm, Suburban House with a Basement</h3>

          <p>
            The risk here is a thunderstorm that knocks out power while rain is still falling, so the sump pump matters as much as the fridge. The daily list: refrigerator 1,500Wh; sump pump, a 1/3 HP unit at about 800W running for ten seconds every five minutes during heavy inflow, which works out to roughly 650Wh across a wet day; internet 20W around the clock, 480Wh; CPAP 40W for eight hours, 320Wh; LED lights 200Wh; phone charging 100Wh. That totals about 3,250Wh a day, or roughly 3,820Wh of battery once losses are included.
          </p>

          <p>
            Output: fridge running 200W, internet 20W, CPAP 40W, lights 60W, plus the sump pump's 800W running and a starting surge of 1,300W or more. Worst case, with the fridge compressor starting at the same instant, you are looking at 2,500 to 3,000W for a moment. A 4kWh unit with 4,000W or more of output handles this for about one full day, and a 2kWh unit gets through roughly half a day, enough for a typical storm outage but tight if the power stays off overnight and the rain continues. If the basement floods regularly, a dedicated battery backup sump pump is worth considering so the pump never depends on the power station at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 2: Winter Outage, Gas Furnace</h3>

          <p>
            A gas furnace still needs electricity: the blower, the igniter, the inducer fan and the control board all stop without it. In a cold snap the blower may run for ten hours a day or more. Using a 400W blower as a middle-of-the-range figure, that is 4,000Wh a day on its own. Add the fridge at 1,200Wh, since it works less in a cool house, internet 480Wh, CPAP 320Wh, lights 300Wh in the longer evenings and charging 100Wh, and the total is about 6,400Wh, or roughly 7,500Wh of battery a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is nearly double what a 4kWh unit holds. In practice it means one of three things: a base unit plus one or more add-on batteries; a plan to recharge from a generator once a day, because winter sun is weak; or turning the thermostat down to the low 60s, which can shorten blower run time substantially. The furnace is hardwired, so this household needs a transfer switch, interlock or smart panel regardless of which battery it buys. Newer furnaces with variable-speed blower motors draw considerably less than older ones, so measure yours rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 3: Rural House on a 240V Well</h3>

          <p>
            Here energy is not the hard part; output is. A family rationing water to around 100 gallons a day with a pump moving about 10 gallons a minute runs the pump for perhaps ten minutes a day. At roughly 1,200W running, that is only about 200Wh. But the starting surge of a 240V submersible pump can be several times its running draw, and it arrives without warning whenever the pressure tank drops. Add a fridge at 1,500Wh, a chest freezer at 900Wh, internet 480Wh and lights and charging 300Wh, and the daily total is around 3,400Wh, or about 4,000Wh of battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 4kWh class unit therefore lasts about a day, but only a model with native 240V output and plenty of surge capacity can start the pump reliably. That points to the Anker SOLIX F3800 first and the EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 second, connected through a transfer switch that includes the well-pump circuit. Confirm the pump's starting current before buying; it is the number that decides whether this plan works.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What These Examples Mean in Hours</h3>

