        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Connecting to the House Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plug-in loads are easy: run a heavy-duty extension cord from the power station to the fridge, or put the station right next to it. Because a battery produces no exhaust, it can sit in the kitchen. Hardwired loads, meaning furnaces, many sump pumps, well pumps and lighting circuits, are different. There are three safe ways to reach them, and one dangerous one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER BACKFEED</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A double-ended cord into a wall outlet can kill a line worker.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Plugging a power station into a wall outlet with a so-called suicide cord sends power backward into your panel and, unless the main breaker is off, out onto the utility lines. It also bypasses the breaker protection on the circuit and leaves live, exposed prongs on the other end of the cord. It is illegal in most jurisdictions and never worth it.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manual Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch is a small subpanel mounted next to your main panel. An electrician moves a handful of circuits, typically six to ten, into it, and each one gets a switch that selects grid or backup. A power inlet box on the outside wall or in the garage accepts a cord from the power station. During an outage you plug in, flip the selected circuits to backup, and those circuits run from the battery while the grid side is physically disconnected. It is simple, foolproof, and forces you to decide in advance which circuits matter, which is exactly the planning this guide recommends.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Panel Interlock Kit</h3>

          <p>
            An interlock is a sliding plate fitted to the main panel that makes it mechanically impossible to turn on the main breaker and a backfeed breaker at the same time. With an inlet box wired to that backfeed breaker, the whole panel can be powered from backup once the main is off. It is often the lower-cost option and lets you choose circuits on the fly, but that flexibility is also its risk with a battery: switch on the water heater or the range by mistake and you will drain or trip the station in minutes. Treat the interlock as a whole-panel connection that you manage by turning off every non-essential breaker first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manufacturer Smart Panels</h3>

          <p>
            EcoFlow and Anker both sell home panels designed for their large units. These sit alongside your main panel, switch selected circuits automatically when the grid fails, and let you monitor and shed loads from an app. They are the most seamless option, since the house changes over without anyone going to the garage, and the most expensive, and they tie you to one brand's ecosystem. Whichever connection you choose, it is a job for a licensed electrician with a permit, and your utility may need to be told.
          </p>

