        <div id="medical" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. UPS Switchover and Medical Devices
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a CPAP, an oxygen concentrator or a home dialysis setup, the question is not just how long the battery lasts but what happens in the instant the grid drops. Most current power stations offer an uninterruptible power supply mode: the device is plugged into the station, the station is plugged into the wall, and wall power passes through until it fails. Manufacturers typically quote a switchover of roughly 10 to 30 milliseconds, fast enough that most CPAP machines, routers and desktop computers keep running without a reboot.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are caveats worth taking seriously. This pass-through switchover is not the same as a true online UPS, which runs the load from its inverter at all times and has no switchover gap. Some sensitive equipment is less tolerant than a CPAP, so check the device manual or ask the supplier. And a station sitting permanently on pass-through should be one designed for it; the manufacturers that advertise a UPS function intend it for this use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Energy planning for medical devices is straightforward but often overlooked. A CPAP without its heated humidifier uses roughly 30 to 60W, so a 1kWh unit covers two or three nights. Turn the humidifier on and consumption can climb steeply. Stationary oxygen concentrators draw considerably more, often several hundred watts continuously, so a 1kWh unit may last only a few hours; size those with care and talk to the equipment provider about backup cylinders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Register with your utility's medical-needs or priority restoration program if one exists, and keep a written list of each device's wattage taped near the power station so anyone in the house can make the right call at 2 a.m. A compact unit such as the Bluetti Elite 100 V2 or Delta 3 Plus parked permanently at the bedside on pass-through is often the simplest arrangement, leaving the big wheeled unit free for the fridge and the essential circuits.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">RUN CPAP FROM DC</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Skip the inverter and gain runtime.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Many CPAP makers sell a 12V or DC power adapter. Running the machine from the power station's DC output skips the inverter's conversion losses and can add meaningfully to overnight runtime.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="well-pumps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 240V Well Pumps
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your water comes from a well, your water is electric. No power means no flushing, no washing and no drinking water once the pressure tank empties. Many deep-well submersible pumps run on 240V split-phase power, which rules out every 120V-only power station regardless of capacity. Shallow-well jet pumps and some smaller submersibles are 120V; the pump's nameplate or your well installer will tell you which you have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three numbers matter: the pump's voltage, its running current, and its locked-rotor or starting current. The starting surge is the one that trips inverters, because a battery's inverter cannot momentarily overload the way a large spinning generator can. That is why output, not capacity, is the constraint for well homes, and why the 6,000W SOLIX F3800 leads our picks for them. The Delta Pro 3's 4,000W, 240V output covers many residential pumps as well; check the starting figures against each maker's surge specification before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practical strategies help a lot. Ask about a soft-start kit for the pump, which ramps the motor up and can substantially reduce its starting surge. Size the pressure tank generously so the pump cycles less often. During an outage, ration water use and avoid running the pump at the same moment as other motor loads. And make sure the well-pump breaker is one of the circuits wired into your transfer switch, because it is surprisingly common for it to be left off.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your pump's starting current is beyond what any portable unit can supply, that is not a failure of planning; it is an answer. A large deep-well pump may simply be a job for a fuel generator, and a combined plan, with a generator for the pump and a battery for everything quiet, is common in rural homes. Our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> covers the units with enough starting capacity for big motors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="multi-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Multi-Day Outage Strategy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hurricanes, ice storms and wildfire shutoffs regularly leave homes without power for three days or more. No battery you can reasonably buy stores a week of normal living, so a multi-day plan has three parts: cut consumption, recharge every day, and add storage where it makes sense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ration the Big Loads</h3>

          <p>
            The refrigerator is the largest flexible load. A closed, full fridge keeps food safe for about four hours without power, and a full freezer for up to two days. Instead of running the fridge continuously, run it for an hour or two, then unplug it for a few hours, and keep the door closed. Depending on the fridge and the kitchen temperature, cycling like this can cut its draw by roughly a third. Move the most perishable food into a cooler, consolidate the freezer, and keep a thermometer in both compartments so you know rather than guess. Turn off the AC inverter when nothing needs it, because even an idle inverter draws power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recharge from Solar</h3>

          <p>
            Solar is what turns a battery from a countdown into a cycle. Portable panels typically deliver around 60 to 75 percent of their rated wattage in real conditions, and a good day gives roughly four to five peak sun hours. Using 65 percent and 4.5 hours as a middle estimate, 400W of panels puts back about 1,170Wh a day, 800W about 2,340Wh, and 1,200W about 3,500Wh. Pair 1,200W of panels with a 4kWh station and a summer-storm household can, on clear days, run its essentials more or less indefinitely. Cloudy days cut that sharply, so treat solar as a strong supplement rather than a guarantee. Our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> covers panel bundles and input limits in more detail.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Panel wattage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Clear day (~65% x 4.5 h)</th>
