            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recharge from a Generator</h3>

          <p>
            The most robust long-outage setup combines a battery and a fuel generator. Run a small inverter generator outdoors for two or three hours in the afternoon to recharge the power station through its AC charging input, then shut it off and let the battery carry the house silently overnight. You burn a fraction of the gasoline that running the generator around the clock would need, you sleep without engine noise, and the battery handles the brief, peaky loads that generators run inefficiently. Always keep the generator outside, at least 20 feet from doors and windows, and never in a garage, even with the door open.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Storage Before You Need It</h3>

          <p>
            If your outages routinely run past a day, expandability is the feature to pay for. The Delta Pro 3, SOLIX F3800 and Delta 3 Plus all accept their makers' add-on batteries, which add capacity without buying a second inverter. A fixed unit can still be part of a multi-day plan, as a second compact station at the bedside for example, but it cannot grow. Keep a couple of <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for phones</a> as well, so your main battery is not spending its energy on USB charging through the inverter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A THREE-DAY PLAN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Run, recharge, repeat, and shed.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Day one, run essentials from a full 4kWh battery and cycle the fridge. Day two, deploy solar panels at first light and, if skies are gray, run a generator for two to three hours in the afternoon to top up. Day three, repeat, and shed anything not in tiers one to three. Charge every phone and power bank while the battery is being recharged, not from the inverter at night.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Winter Outages and Cold Charging
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winter is the hardest season for battery backup, for four reasons. Heating loads are large. Days are short and solar yields fall. Storms that bring down power lines often bring cloud cover too. And lithium batteries do not like the cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            LFP cells should not be charged below freezing. Charging them under about 32 degrees F (0 degrees C) can permanently damage the cells, and most units' battery management systems block charging in those temperatures to protect them. Discharging in the cold is permitted, but usable capacity drops. The practical rule is simple: keep the power station indoors in winter, in a heated part of the house. A unit stored in an unheated garage may refuse to charge just when you need it, and you will have to bring it inside and let it warm up first. If your transfer-switch inlet is in the garage, store the station inside and roll it out to plug in.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the load side, attack the furnace blower first. Lower the thermostat into the low 60s, close off unused rooms, and let the sun in on south-facing windows during the day. Keep the furnace blower as the only heating load on the battery; an electric space heater drains a 1kWh battery in well under an hour and should not be part of a battery plan. Protect pipes in exterior walls by opening cabinet doors and, if needed, letting a tap drip, but on a well, remember every drip costs pump energy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, plan your recharge around the weather. Winter sun is weak and low; tilting portable panels steeply toward the south helps, but expect much less than summer output. In a long winter outage, a generator recharge is usually the backbone of the plan, with solar as a bonus on clear days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between outages, the station needs very little. Keep it at 50 to 80 percent charge for long storage, top it up every few months, and run a short rehearsal each autumn: plug in at the inlet, flip the transfer switch, and confirm the furnace, fridge and pump all start. Ten minutes on a mild October afternoon is far better than finding a tripped breaker or a missing cord during the first ice storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-standby" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Power Station vs. Standby Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A permanently installed standby generator, running on natural gas or propane, is still the benchmark for whole-house backup. It starts automatically, runs for days on piped gas, and can power central air conditioning and electric ranges. A battery system wins on silence, indoor safety, near-zero maintenance and the ability to work with solar every day, not just in emergencies. The right choice depends on how long your outages last and what you need to run.
          </p>

