                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For households whose outages are usually under a day, a 4kWh battery with a transfer switch delivers most of what a standby generator does for less money, with no fuel to manage. For homes that see multi-day outages every winter, rely on electric heat, or need central air for health reasons, a standby generator is still the more complete answer, and our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">standby generator cost guide</a> breaks down what installation involves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many owners split the difference: a battery for silent, instant backup on essential circuits, and a portable generator to recharge it when an outage drags on. That hybrid costs less than a standby unit, uses a fraction of the fuel of a generator alone, and keeps the house quiet at night. It also degrades gracefully: if the generator will not start, the battery still covers the first night, and if the battery runs low, the generator refills it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The deciding question is usually not which technology is better but which outages you actually get. Look back at the last three years. If every outage was a few hours, a 2kWh plug-in unit is enough. If they ran a day, a 4kWh panel-connected system fits. If they ran most of a week in January, start with a generator and add a battery later for quiet nights.
          </p>
        </div>

