        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by capacity alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big watt-hour number does nothing if the inverter cannot start your hardest load. Add the running watts of everything that might be on together, then the largest single starting surge, and check that sum against both the continuous and surge ratings before you compare capacity at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a wall outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-ended cord from the station to an outlet energizes your panel and potentially the utility lines, where it can kill a line worker. It also bypasses the circuit's breaker protection. Use a transfer switch, an interlock with an inlet, or the maker's smart panel, installed by a licensed electrician.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the station in a freezing garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                LFP cells should not be charged below about 32 degrees F, and most battery management systems block charging in the cold. A unit parked in an unheated garage may refuse to recharge on the morning you need it. Keep it in a heated room and wheel it to the inlet when an outage starts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the well-pump circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is surprisingly common for the well-pump breaker to be left out of the transfer switch, or for a 120V-only station to be bought for a 240V pump. Check the pump's voltage and starting current on the nameplate, and confirm the circuit is wired in before the first outage arrives.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging phones through the inverter all night</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every USB charge routed through the AC inverter loses energy to conversion and keeps an idle inverter drawing power. Charge phones and power banks while the station itself is recharging, use its DC ports when you can, and keep a separate portable charger so the main battery stays on critical loads.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station back up my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can back up the parts that matter most. A 4kWh class unit such as the EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 or Anker SOLIX F3800, connected through a transfer switch, interlock or smart panel, can run a fridge, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, internet, lights and medical devices for roughly a day, longer with solar or add-on batteries. It will not sensibly run central air, electric heat or an electric range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a battery do I need for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up the daily energy of everything on your critical-load list, then divide by 0.85 for losses. A fridge, internet, CPAP and lights typically need about 2,500Wh a day, so roughly 3,000Wh of battery. A sump pump in a storm pushes that toward 4,000Wh, and a furnace blower in cold weather toward 7,000Wh. As a rule of thumb, 1kWh is an hours-long bridge, 2kWh covers a night and 4kWh covers about a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery generator better than a gas generator for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Each wins in different situations. A battery generator is silent, produces no carbon monoxide, needs almost no maintenance and recharges from solar, which suits short outages, medical devices and apartments. A gas generator stores far more energy in a can of fuel, so it is better for long outages and heavy loads. For multi-day outages the strongest setup is both: a generator recharges the battery in the afternoon, and the battery runs the house overnight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for plug-in appliances: a fridge, CPAP, router or lamp can plug straight into the station or into a heavy-duty extension cord. You do need a transfer switch, a panel interlock with an inlet, or the maker's smart panel for hardwired loads such as a furnace blower, well pump or lighting circuits. Never backfeed through a wall outlet, and have a licensed electrician install the connection, with a permit where required.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power station run a 240V well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a unit with native 240V split-phase output and enough surge capacity to start the motor. Among these picks, the Anker SOLIX F3800 at 6,000W and the EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 at 4,000W both offer 120/240V. The deciding number is the pump's starting current, which can be several times its running draw. Get it from the nameplate or your well installer, consider a soft-start kit, and wire the pump circuit into your transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a power station as a UPS for a CPAP machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in most cases. Many current stations have a UPS or pass-through mode that switches to battery within about 10 to 30 milliseconds by the manufacturers' figures, which is fast enough for most CPAP machines; check your device manual if unsure. Without the heated humidifier a CPAP uses about 30 to 60W, so a 1kWh unit lasts two or three nights, and a DC adapter from the CPAP maker stretches that further.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I keep a power station in my garage for winter backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is better kept indoors in a heated space. LFP batteries should not be charged below about 32 degrees F, and most units block charging in the cold, so a station stored in a freezing garage may refuse to recharge just when you need it. If your transfer-switch inlet is in the garage, store the unit inside and wheel it out when an outage starts. For storage, keep it at 50 to 80 percent and top it up every few months.
              </p>
            </div>

